      "marks": 10,
      "gold_answer": "Because the rocking chairs are indistinguishable from each other and the stools are\nindistinguishable from each other, we can think of first placing the two stools somewhere\nin the ten slots and then filling the rest with rocking chairs. The first stool has $10$\nslots in which it can go, and the second has $9$. However, because they are\nindistinguishable from each other, we have overcounted the number of ways to place the\nstools by a factor of $2$, so we divide by $2$. Thus, there are $\\frac{10 \\cdot 9}{2} =\n\\boxed{45}$ distinct ways to arrange the ten chairs and stools for a meeting.",
